UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 26, Lesson 1: Australian music and its history', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Australian music includes many histories: songs of community life, classical and popular works composed here, migrant musical traditions and First Nations music cultures. When studying history context, use published timelines and classroom-approved recordings. Describe instruments, purpose and place with evidence from what you hear, not stereotypes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nGive two listening features you can cite as evidence and one research rule for Australian music history.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Tempo and instrumentation as evidence. Rule: use approved sources and credit them; avoid stereotypes.''\n\nWATCH FOR\nClaiming one style is the only Australian music. Many traditions coexist.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nListen to two teacher-approved Australian pieces from different times or communities. Complete a compare chart: purpose, instruments, tempo, source credit.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Australian music includes many histories: songs of community life, classical and popular works composed here, migrant musical traditions and First Nations music cultures. When studying history context, use published timelines and classroom-approved recordings. Describe instruments, purpose and place with evidence from what you hear, not stereotypes.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-MUS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Listen to two teacher-approved Australian pieces from different times or communities. Complete a compare chart: purpose, instruments, tempo, source credit. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using Australian music on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Claiming one style is the only Australian music.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Tempo and instrumentation as evidence.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Claiming one style is the only Australian music. Many traditions coexist. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating Australian music on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with Australian music, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about Australian music with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for Australian music. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for Australian music. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","Tempo and instrumentation as evidence.","Claiming one style is the only Australian music."],"question":"Give two listening features you can cite as evidence and one research rule for Australian music history. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for Australian music; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Tempo and instrumentation as evidence.","Careful use of history context and explain why it matte with clear evidence.","Claiming one style is the only Australian music.","Australian music includes many histories: songs of community life, classical and popular works composed here, migrant"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with Australian music?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named history context and explain why it matte, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply Australian music to new material and name the history context and explain why it matte used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Claiming one style is the only Australian music.","Name history context and explain why it matte only, without linking it to Australian music."],"question":"Which response best applies Australian music with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on Australian music. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Claiming one style is the only Australian music.","Tempo and instrumentation as evidence.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for Australian music?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Claiming one style is the only Australian music.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for Australian music."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about Australian music?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Claiming one style is the only Australian music.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Example: Students compare an approved recording of a community folk song with a modern school band piece, noting"],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Australian music includes many histories: songs of community life, classical and popular works — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for Australian music?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Claiming one style is the only Australian music.","Name history context and explain why it matte only, with no linked example.","Listen to two teacher-approved Australian pieces from different times or communities."],"question":"Which independent response best applies Australian music to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about Australian music with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating Australian music on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using Australian music on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for Australian music. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with Australian music, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Claiming one style is the only Australian music.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Tempo and instrumentation as evidence.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w26-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise Australian music.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use Australian music with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify history context and explain why it matters.","I can practise genre independently on a new prompt.","I can notice listening evidence needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Australian music includes many histories: songs of community life, classical and popular works composed here, migrant musical traditions and First Nations music cultures.","options":["Australian music includes many histories: songs of community life, classical and popular works composed here, migrant","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about Australian music with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Students compare an approved recording of a community folk song with a modern school band piece, noting acoustic versus mixed instruments and different purposes (gathering versus concert).\n\nTRY FIRST\nGive two listening features you can cite as evidence and one research rule for Australian music history.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Tempo and instrumentation as evidence. Rule: use approved sources and credit them; avoid stereotypes.''","explain":"Australian music includes many histories: songs of community life, classical and popular works composed here, migrant musical traditions and First Nations music cultures. When studying history context, use published timelines and classroom-approved recordings. Describe instruments, purpose and place with evidence from what you hear, not stereotypes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Claiming one style is the only Australian music. Many traditions coexist. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Australian music listening.","caption":"Cite what you hear.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Context</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">time place purpose</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Evidence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">instruments tempo</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Students compare an approved recording of a community folk song with a modern school band piece, noting acousti Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Students compare an approved recording of a community folk song with a modern school band piece, noting acousti Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about Australian music with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Students compare an approved recording of a community folk song with a modern school band piece, noting acoustic versus mixed instruments and different purposes (gathering versus concert). Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Research rules.","caption":"Many traditions.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Sources</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">approved credited</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Avoid</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">stereotypes</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about Australian music with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nClaiming one style is the only Australian music. Many traditions coexist. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Students compare an approved recording of a community folk song with a modern school band piece, noting acoustic versus mixed instruments and different purposes (gathering versus concert).\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Tempo and instrumentation as evidence. Rule: use approved sources and credit them; avoid stereotypes.''","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Claiming one style is the only Australian music. Many traditions coexist. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about Australian music with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAustralian music includes many histories: songs of community life, classical and popular works composed here, migrant musical traditions and First Nations music cultures. When studying history context, use published timelines and classroom-approved recordings. Describe instruments, purpose and place with evidence from what you hear, not stereotypes. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 26, Lesson 1: Australian music and its history","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Composing and Australian music","week":26,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"work